+# The first lines of every script a caller starts (zn, the gates, the forwarders, ac.sh) source this and call
+#   pickman_clean_env "$0" "$@"
+# (c0123). When the exported environment is not the allow-list of cleanenv.list, or PATH not the fixed one, the script
+# starts again, in place, under `env -i` with exactly the allowed names and the fixed PATH. A clean environment returns
+# after the checks (about 15 ms). Only builtins and absolute paths are used before that (PATH is not trusted until the end). Start the script
+# with `#!/bin/bash -p`: privileged mode runs no $BASH_ENV and imports no shell function, which no line of the script
+# can undo afterwards (an imported function can shadow even `unset` and `command`). A bash the caller started without
+# -p has run its $BASH_ENV before our first line: when that can be seen (BASH_ENV or ENV set) this refuses.
+# The restart carries PICKMAN_CLEANED=<its own pid> (an exec keeps the pid): a restarted shell that is still dirty
+# refuses instead of looping, and a value forged by the caller can only make it refuse.
+# What the run itself puts on PATH (stubs, the run's temp bin, the browser guard's folder) is believed by where it is,
+# never by a variable or by how its path reads: its canonical path (`cd -P`, then the filesystem's own spelling from
+# /bin/pwd -P) must lie inside a folder the runner registered for this run (tests-run.lock/folders, written by the
+# runner in a `.cache` of the checkout that holds this file, found from this file's own location), and its name must
+# match an `internal` glob. The registered folder counts only while the run that registered it holds it (its
+# .pickman-owner names a live process that is not a reused pid), so a killed run's registry proves nothing. The run's
+# `fence` names pass only while such a folder matching a `proof` glob is on PATH.
+_pickman_clean_list="${BASH_SOURCE[0]%/*}/cleanenv.list"
+_pickman_pwd=/bin/pwd
+[[ -x $_pickman_pwd ]] || _pickman_pwd=/usr/bin/pwd
